s the villainous  and ambitious director chronic and  stands out visually thanks to excellent  work in the costume department by  designers David Crossman and Glenn  Dillon in the role of Jin [ __ ] father  Galen Mads Mikkelsen brings out his  character strengths and suffering even  during moments of exposition after  seeing the trailers I've got to admit I  was a bit worried about Forest  Whitaker's liners outlaw rebels saw  Guerrera save the rebellion save the  dream which every time I saw it at the  cinema screamed heroes save the  cheerleader save the world to me  and not in a good way so I was glad to  see that it plays out in a much less  cheesy way in the final movie and it's  actually said in what I felt was a much  more plaintive tone seek context is  everything  Oh in a certain character from the  previous films returns to give you  chills I really liked the way he's  introduced when we see him fully and I  was fine with the amount he appears in  the film too with so many new characters  and locations to introduce rogue one  does take its time to build up a full  head of steam and there is some naughty  plotting and certain inelegant character  shifts which means it's a while before  things get going with the meat of the  movie of course it's been a lot of talk  of long reshoots and just how much of  chris fights his script was reworked by  the Bourne movies Tony Gilroy how many  of those things fed into some of the  film's issues and how many strengths and  it is difficult to say with absolute  certainty the film I'm reviewing here is  obviously the one I saw in front of me  on the big screen and in this rogue one  director Gareth Edwards has made a bold  film that's dark in tone the previous  Star Wars outings that does not shy away  from the complexities and violence of  warfare and that introduces conflicted  characters many of whom work their way  right into my affections and when the  film is at its best we find ourselves in  the middle of a heist and a war movie  with vibes of Saving Private Ryan Meets  The Dirty Dozen via Black Hawk Down and  The Hurt Locker in terms of spectacle  rogue one is dazzling  there are so many heart-stopping and  staring scenes in the movie that brought  tears to my eyes and they build up to an  all-encompassing incredible crescendo  and as the film moves into its final  phase its editors keep nifty cutting  between a variety of characters and  locations ramping up the tension  cinematographer Greg Fraser who's known  for zero-dark says he takes us right  into the middle of the action getting  down and dirty to full gritty effect  making us feel every blast and explosion  as he weaves between obstacles and  enemies just as our heroes do amid the  dust smoke and sand during action scenes  the camera is fluid and often handheld  and apparently director Edwards  regularly operated it himself so kudos  also goes to him for the film's final  look and for also managing to give us  both amazing scale in the film while  still often keeping action grounded on a  more personal level Edwards and  cinematographer Fraser also  make great use of the various locations  where they filmed including the Maldives  for example they really bring out the  contrast between the beautiful beachside  settings and the full-on horror of war  as things are blown to smithereens  beside sapphire waters and perfectly  matching the movie's naturalistic  cinematography is Doug Chang and Neil  Lamont's production design which brings  out the realistically rough edges of the  Star Wars world for some superb stunt  choreography and some truly explosive  scenes props also go to stunt  coordinator Rob inch he worked on the  force awakens and captain Phillips and  two oscar-winning special effects  supervisor Neil kobold whose affection  of seen in Kingsman and Black Hawk Down  and of course it wouldn't be a Star Wars  movie without some spectacular visual  effects and for those we have to thank  Star Wars veteran John Knoll who also  came up with the original idea for rogue  one as well as Moe and Leo who worked on  Ant Man and the Martian there is some  absolutely stunning moments set out in  space as well as on various planets and  moons and so many of the scenes of  destruction which we see from both gods  and worm's-eye views are obviously  properly devastating but also almost  perversely things of extreme beauty the  only place where I say the VFX were  slightly off was with the face of one  particular character I think they did a  good job getting 80% of the way there in  a tough situation but at first I did  find it distracting though later I got  used to it so it didn't spoil the movie  overall for me and of course I've got to  give a shout out to BAFTA winning Neal  Scanlan and his creature effects team  for their work on the movies many new  cool alien life-forms and droids as so  often happens with the Star Wars movies  some of their work just flies by all too  quickly so I can't wait to see it on DVD  so I can take a lingering look over some  of their new creations with his score  for rogue one composer Michael Giacchino  has now completed the full Disney  composing package adding Star Wars to  scores for Disney Animation's utopia  Disney's live-action Tomorrowland  numerous Pixar films and Marvel's Doctor  Strange and what a great job he's done  here - and there's even some lovely  little moments blended in at appropriate  points from John Williams original Star  Wars themes which always makes me feel  like a little kid again  to sum up rogue one is full of both  stunning spectacle and genuinely moving  moments and introduces some engaging new  characters given life  ensemble of accomplished actors though  the film does take a while to rev up and  move through some thorny plot moments  the payoff is so worth it that it's  entirely possible that you leave the  cinema on a super high like I did ro  Guan is the Star Wars prequel I've been  looking for and there's so much the film  gets right that for me it's up there  with the very best Star Wars movies and  its final act is intense  mind blowing cinema and I'm really  looking forward to watching it again so  I'm giving rogue one four and a half out  of 5 flicks now what did you think of  rogue one who are your favorite new  characters and how do you think it  compares to the other Star Wars movies  let me know in the comments below  coming up soon I'll have more rogue one  videos including my Easter Egg Guide to  the movie so be sure to subscribe for  more movie stuff like this and don't  forget to turn on your notifications so  you get my new videos as soon as they're  ready and you can also click or tap here  to check out more of my videos thanks  for watching and see you next time  yippee-ki-yay movie lovers
 

